Wheel scrolling runs on the scrolling thread, as upstream has it with forced
compositing, unless WEBKIT_DISABLE_THREADED_SCROLLING=1 asks for the main
thread.

This patch used to turn threaded scrolling off for good.  With it on, sticky
and fixed headers jumped by the scroll distance and back on every wheel step
(github.com, chatgpt.com, redhat.com) whenever the main thread did not finish
its rendering update within half a frame: the compositor took layer positions
half from the main thread's layer flush and half from the scrolling tree.
Patch 0028 commits those positions as one set, which is what removes the
shaking, so the scrolling thread is back -- and with it scrolling that does
not wait for the main thread: a page whose scripts keep the main thread busy
for a second no longer stops scrolling for that second.  Patch 0029 extends
that to pages with passive wheel listeners (microsoft.com among them), 0030
to pages that were idle when the wheel started, and 0031 keeps the areas the
scroll reaches before the main thread has painted them from showing stale or
undefined pixels.

The variable stays as the way to compare the two on one machine.  Without a
scrolling thread there is no scrolling coordinator either, so a composited
frame must not get the coordinated scrollbars controller, which leaves
drawing the scrollbars to the scrolling tree: the scrollbar would still be
draggable but never drawn.  Such a frame keeps the generic controller, and
the Adwaita theme paints its scrollbars into their layers instead of leaving
them to the (absent) scrolling thread.

